% ============================================================================
% wave-properties — the four measurable properties of a wave and what each one
% is / measures, as the source states it (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% Before you can reason about sound, light, or ocean swells you first learn to
% MEASURE a wave, and a wave is measured along two axes at once. In SPACE it has
% a wavelength (how far apart two identical parts are) and an amplitude (how far
% the medium swings from its rest position). In TIME it has a frequency (how
% many waves go by each second) and a period (how long one full wave takes).
% These four numbers describe any wave completely, and each is a rung a PHYSICS
% agent reasons up from. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? wave_property(wavelength, $Definition)   % distance_between_identical_parts
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `wave_property(property, definition)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% definition WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% a word that is not one of these four measurable wave properties (it never
% invents a definition).
%
% Each measurable property and the definition the source states for it, as a
% little truth table:
%
%     wave property   what the source says it is           axis it lives on
%     -------------   ----------------------------------   --------------------
%     wavelength      distance_between_identical_parts     space (distance)
%     frequency       waves_per_second                     time  (rate)
%     amplitude       max_displacement_from_rest           space (displacement)
%     period          time_for_one_cycle                   time  (duration)
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a definition and recall its property:
%
%     ? wave_property($Property, time_for_one_cycle)   % period
%
% A word that is NOT one of these four measurable wave properties — `color`,
% `speed`, `medium` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a wave-property recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a definition. (Color is a PERCEPTION that
% depends on wavelength; it is not itself a measurable property of the wave, so
% it is intentionally left out.) That honest-abstention property is what makes
% the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the property→
% definition pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `definition` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% faithful compression of the definition the source EXPLICITLY states for that
% property — never one the source does not support. Each atom stays literally
% checkable against the quoted span below (e.g. the source says a wave's period
% is the "time it takes for one wave cycle to complete", so the atom is
% `time_for_one_cycle`, using the source's own words).
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every property→definition
% pair below was read out of a fetched OpenStax Physics page, and any pair whose
% definition could not be verified there would have been dropped). Each pair,
% with the primary source it was copied from and the verbatim span that states
% it:
%
%   wavelength → distance_between_identical_parts
%     https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/13-2-wave-properties-speed-amplitude-frequency-and-period
%     (OpenStax, Physics — Rice University open textbook)
%     "The wavelength λ is the distance between adjacent identical parts of a
%      wave, parallel to the direction of propagation."
%
%   frequency → waves_per_second
%     https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/13-2-wave-properties-speed-amplitude-frequency-and-period
%     (OpenStax, Physics — Rice University open textbook)
%     "Frequency—number of waves passing by a specific point per second"
%
%   amplitude → max_displacement_from_rest
%     https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/13-2-wave-properties-speed-amplitude-frequency-and-period
%     (OpenStax, Physics — Rice University open textbook)
%     "Amplitude—distance between the resting position and the maximum
%      displacement of the wave"
%
%   period → time_for_one_cycle
%     https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/13-2-wave-properties-speed-amplitude-frequency-and-period
%     (OpenStax, Physics — Rice University open textbook)
%     "Period—time it takes for one wave cycle to complete"
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the OpenStax statement that fixes
% the first row (wavelength → distance_between_identical_parts). Every OTHER
% row's per-fact source and verbatim span is listed above, so each definition
% remains independently checkable. All four rows come from the same OpenStax
% Physics page — a peer-reviewed open textbook (a teaching summary), so `trust
% consensus`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table wave_property {
    columns property, definition

    row (wavelength, distance_between_identical_parts)
    row (frequency, waves_per_second)
    row (amplitude, max_displacement_from_rest)
    row (period, time_for_one_cycle)

    source "The wavelength λ is the distance between adjacent identical parts of a wave, parallel to the direction of propagation."
    locator "https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/13-2-wave-properties-speed-amplitude-frequency-and-period"
    trust consensus
}
